@page "/bootstrap"

<PageTitle>Fitting a UI library</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="lib/bootstrap.min.css" />
</HeadContent>
<h1>Fitting a UI library</h1>
<p>The same Quickstart model and validator, dressed in Bootstrap. The whole integration is one <code>Options</code> object: <code>FormidableCssClasses</code> maps the state class NAMES onto Bootstrap's, and the markup uses Bootstrap's own classes. While you are on this page Bootstrap's stylesheet is loaded globally — that is how single-page WASM head links work. In light mode that gives the rest of this page a slightly Bootstrap-flavoured look too; in dark mode the site shell holds onto its own colours. The demo below also follows your OS colour scheme — Bootstrap themes itself with one attribute, <code>data-bs-theme</code>, which a few lines of page script keep in sync.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "BootstrapFitting.razor", "BootstrapFitting.razor.cs", "QuickContact.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Name</strong> — required.</li>
            <li><strong>Email</strong> — required and valid (one message at a time). Identical validator to Quickstart.</li>
            <li>The seam: <code>CssClasses = new() { Invalid = "is-invalid", Valid = "is-valid" }</code> — Formidable puts Bootstrap's own state classes on the inputs; Bootstrap's stylesheet does the rest.</li>
            <li>Bootstrap has no warning/info tier of its own to remap onto, so <code>Warning</code> and <code>Info</code> are left at Formidable's own defaults here — invisible on this page, since neither field's validator ever raises one.</li>
            <li>Message and summary components keep their <code>formidable-*</code> structural classes — your stylesheet styles those (here the site theme still does).</li>
            <li>Dark mode: the wrapper's <code>data-bs-theme</code> attribute is toggled to match your OS by page script — Bootstrap's own theming hook; the kit is not involved.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit empty — Bootstrap's red <code>is-invalid</code> borders appear, no site-theme CSS involved.</li>
            <li>Fix a field and leave it — Bootstrap's green <code>is-valid</code> state shows.</li>
            <li>Open <em>Show the code</em> — the only Formidable-specific lines are the <code>Options</code> object and the components themselves.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<div id="bootstrap-demo" class="bootstrap-demo p-3 border rounded">
    <FormidableForm Model="_contact" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
        <div class="summary-slot">
            <FormidableSummary />
        </div>

        <div class="mb-3"><label class="form-label">Name <FormidableInputText class="form-control" @bind-Value="_contact.Name" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _contact.Name" /></div>
        <div class="mb-3"><label class="form-label">Email <FormidableInputText class="form-control" @bind-Value="_contact.Email" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _contact.Email" /></div>

        <div class="actions"><button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Submit</button></div>
    </FormidableForm>
</div>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
